{"name":"Silver Sand","hex":"#dadedd","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver sand","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fine white sand used in gardening."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver_sand"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver Sand","blocks":[{"text":"Silver Sand is an open-pit silver mine project in Potosí Department, central Bolivia."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Sand","wikidata":"Q139961386"},{"title":"Silver sand","blocks":[{"text":"Silver sand is a fine white sand used in gardening. It consists largely of quartz particles that are not coated with iron oxides. Iron oxides colour sand from yellows to rich browns. Silver sand is also used as a constituent of mortar for laying light coloured pavers."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_sand","wikidata":"Q23583051"},{"title":"Sand","blocks":[{"text":"Sand is a granular material composed of finely divided mineral particles. Sand has various compositions but is usually defined by its grain size. Sand grains are smaller than gravel and coarser than silt. Sand can also refer to a textural class of soil or soil type; i.e., a soil containing more than 85 percent sand-sized particles by mass."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Libya_4608_Idehan_Ubari_Dunes_Luca_Galuzzi_2007.jpg/330px-Libya_4608_Idehan_Ubari_Dunes_Luca_Galuzzi_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sand","wikidata":"Q34679"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver","wikidata":"Q1090"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/00065_sand_collage.jpg/500px-00065_sand_collage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/00065_sand_collage.jpg/1280px-00065_sand_collage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/00065_sand_collage.jpg/500px-00065_sand_collage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Depiction of sands: glass, dune, quartz, volcanic, biogenic coral, pink coral,volcanic, garnet, olivine.Samples are from the Gobi Desert, Estonia, Hawaii and the mainland United States. (1×1 cm each)[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A00065_sand_collage.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/HeavyMineralsBeachSand.jpg/500px-HeavyMineralsBeachSand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/HeavyMineralsBeachSand.jpg/1280px-HeavyMineralsBeachSand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/HeavyMineralsBeachSand.jpg/500px-HeavyMineralsBeachSand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Heavy minerals (dark) in a quartz beach sand (Chennai, India)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHeavyMineralsBeachSand.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/CoralPinkSandDune